lessons.text:
This lesson gets Java running on your computer and teaches you to write, compile, and run your first program. By the end you will know how to check whether Java is already installed, how to install a Java development kit if it is not, how to save a program in a plain text file, and how to compile it and run it from the command line. You will also learn the basic shape of every Java program. Run every example yourself as you read, because setup only becomes real when you see your own program print its first line.

First, check whether Java is already installed. Open a terminal or command prompt and type java -version, then press Enter. If you see a version number, Java is present. Also type javac -version and press Enter. The javac command is the compiler: it turns the code you write into a form the computer can run. You need both commands to work for this course. If either one is not recognized, you need to install a Java development kit, which includes both the runtime and the compiler. Download a current long-term-support release from the official Java download page or from your operating system's package manager, run the installer, and then close and reopen your terminal so it picks up the new commands. If the commands still are not recognized after installing, the installer may not have added Java to your system path; repeat the install step carefully, or check the installer's help page for your operating system.

With Java installed, write your first program. Open a plain text editor, type the following code exactly, and save the file as Hello.java. The file name must match the class name inside the program, including the capital H:

public class Hello {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Hello, world!");
        System.out.println("Welcome to Java.");
    }
}

Now, in your terminal, move to the folder where you saved the file and type javac Hello.java, then press Enter. If there are no errors, this creates a file called Hello.class in the same folder. Then type java Hello and press Enter. Both lines print. Notice that you compile with the file name including .java, but you run with just the class name, no extension. That distinction trips up many beginners, so say it to yourself as you type: compile the file, run the class.

Let us read the program line by line so none of it feels like magic. The first line, public class Hello, declares a class named Hello. In Java, all code lives inside classes, and a class is a blueprint that groups related code together. The second line, public static void main(String[] args), declares the main method: the starting point of the program. When you run java Hello, Java looks for this exact method and starts there. The words public, static, and void are part of the required spelling for now; you will understand each one properly in later lessons, and for now you can treat the whole main line as a fixed pattern you copy. Inside main, the two lines beginning System.out.println print text to the screen. The text is wrapped in double quotes, and each statement ends with a semicolon. Curly braces mark the beginning and end of the class and the method, and indentation is just for human readability: Java does not care about it, but your future self does.

Two beginner mistakes cause most Lesson 1 frustration. First, saving the file with the wrong name or extension: Hello.java must end in .java, not .txt, and the class name inside must match the file name exactly, including capital letters. If you save as hello.java with a lowercase h while the class is Hello, the compiler will complain. Second, typing java Hello.java to run the program: that tries to run a source file as if it were a class, and it fails. Compile with javac Hello.java, run with java Hello. When something fails, read the error message from the top: the compiler usually names the file and line number, and a missing semicolon on one line is often reported on the next line, so check the line above the reported one too.

Practice: Create a file called AboutMe.java with a class named AboutMe and a main method. Inside main, use three System.out.println statements to print your name, your favorite hobby, and one thing you want to build with Java. Compile it with javac AboutMe.java and run it with java AboutMe. Then, on purpose, remove one semicolon, compile again, and read the error message. Put the semicolon back, compile, and confirm it works. Finally, try running java AboutMe.java and read the error, so you recognize that mistake instantly if you make it again.

This lesson teaches you variables: named boxes that store values your program can use and change. By the end you will know the main Java types for whole numbers, decimal numbers, text, single characters, and true-or-false values; you will declare variables, assign values, combine them with operators, and print results. You will also learn how Java's strict typing protects you and where it catches beginners out. Variables appear in every program you will ever write, so make sure each example below runs on your machine before moving on.

A variable has a type, a name, and a value. The type says what kind of value it can hold, the name is how you refer to it, and the value is what it currently holds. You declare a variable by writing its type, then its name, then an equals sign and a value, ending with a semicolon:

int age = 30;
double price = 12.50;
String name = "Maya";
char grade = 'A';
boolean isOpen = true;

The int type holds whole numbers like 30, -3, and 1000. The double type holds numbers with a decimal point like 12.50 and 98.6. The String type, with a capital S, holds text in double quotes. The char type holds a single character in single quotes, like 'A' or '7'. The boolean type holds only true or false, written in lowercase. You can also declare first and assign later: int score; on one line, then score = 85; on a later line. But you must assign a value before you use a variable, or the compiler will stop you with an error saying the variable might not have been initialized. That is Java protecting you from using a value that was never set.

Java is statically typed, which means each variable keeps its type forever. Once you declare int age, age can only hold whole numbers. Trying to store text in it, like age = "thirty";, is a compile error, not a surprise at runtime. This strictness feels fussy at first, but it catches a whole category of mistakes before your program ever runs. There are two related types worth knowing now: long for very large whole numbers, and float for decimal numbers that use less memory, though double is the usual choice for decimals in beginner code. Type names matter in exact spelling and case: Int and int are different, and only lowercase int is a type. String is the one type name that starts with a capital letter, because it is a class, a fact Lesson 7 will explain.

Operators combine values into new values. Arithmetic operators work like a calculator: + adds, - subtracts, * multiplies, and / divides. Two are special: % gives the remainder after division, so 7 % 2 is 1, and that remainder operator is surprisingly useful for checking even and odd numbers or cycling through positions. Integer division is a classic trap: 7 / 2 in Java is 3, not 3.5, because dividing two ints gives an int and drops the fractional part. If you want 3.5, at least one side must be a double: 7.0 / 2 gives 3.5. The + operator on Strings joins them together: "Good " + "morning" gives "Good morning". You can also join text and numbers: "Age: " + age gives "Age: 30" because Java converts the number to text for the joining. You can change a variable with shorthand: age = age + 1; can be written age++; to add one, and total += 5; adds five to total. These shortcuts are common in real code, so learn to read them even if you write the long form at first.

Two mistakes dominate this lesson. First, using = when you mean ==. A single equals sign stores a value, while a double equals sign compares two values and gives true or false. In Lesson 3 you will write if (age == 18), and writing if (age = 18) instead is an error in Java. Read = as gets, as in age gets 30, and == as equals, and your brain will flag the mix-up. Second, mixing types without converting: String count = "5"; followed by count + 1 does not give 6, it gives the text "51", because + on text joins instead of adding. To turn text into a number you convert explicitly, for example int count = Integer.parseInt("5");. The reverse, turning a number into text, happens automatically when you join it with a String using +, as shown above.

Practice: Write a program called Shop.java that declares variables for an item name (a String), its price (a double), and how many you are buying (an int). Compute the total cost in a new double variable, then print a sentence like "3 notebooks cost 15.0" using the variables, not typed-in numbers. Then add a second program section, or a second small program, that prints the results of 7 / 2, 7.0 / 2, 7 % 2, and 10 % 3, and write a one-line comment in your own words above each print explaining the result. A comment in Java starts with // and runs to the end of the line. Test your program with different prices and quantities and confirm the total changes correctly.

This lesson teaches your programs to make decisions: to choose one path when a condition is true and another when it is false. By the end you will write if, else if, and else statements, combine conditions with logical operators, compare values correctly, and use switch for choosing between many options. Decisions are what turn a program that always does the same thing into a program that responds to its input, and nearly every script you write from now on will use them.

An if statement runs a block of code only when its condition is true:

int age = 20;
if (age >= 18) {
    System.out.println("You are an adult.");
} else {
    System.out.println("You are under 18.");
}

Notice the structure: the word if, then the condition in parentheses, then the code to run inside curly braces. The else block runs when the condition is false. For more than two paths, add else if branches between them:

int score = 85;
if (score >= 90) {
    System.out.println("Grade: A");
} else if (score >= 80) {
    System.out.println("Grade: B");
} else if (score >= 70) {
    System.out.println("Grade: C");
} else {
    System.out.println("Grade: below C");
}

Java checks the branches top to bottom and runs the first one whose condition is true, then skips the rest. So order matters: the highest threshold must come first, because if you checked score >= 70 first, a score of 95 would stop there and be graded C. Each condition must be a boolean value, true or false, or an expression that produces one, such as a comparison. Always write the braces, even for a one-line block: it prevents a classic bug where a second line you add later looks like part of the if but is not.

Comparison operators produce boolean values. The operator == checks equality, != checks inequality, and <, >, <=, >= compare size. So 5 == 5 is true, 5 != 5 is false, and "apple" cannot be compared with < in the way numbers can. One of the most common beginner errors in any language is using a single = where == belongs: age = 18 stores 18 into age, while age == 18 asks whether age equals 18. In Java this mistake usually fails to compile inside an if, which is good, because the compiler catches it. Logical operators combine boolean values: && means and, and is true only when both sides are true; || means or, and is true when either side is true; ! means not, and flips a value. So if (age >= 18 && hasTicket) runs only when both parts are true, and if (isWeekend || isHoliday) runs when either one is true. You can group conditions with parentheses to make the grouping clear: if ((age < 12) || (age >= 65)) gives a discount to children and older adults.

Comparing text needs special care in Java. The == operator on Strings checks whether two variables refer to the same object in memory, not whether the words match, so it can give the wrong answer even when the text looks identical. Always compare String contents with the equals method: name.equals("Maya") returns true when the text matches. There is also equalsIgnoreCase, which ignores capital letters, so "maya".equalsIgnoreCase("Maya") is true. This is one of those rules that feels strange until you have been bitten by it once, and then you never forget it. For numbers, chars, and booleans, == works exactly as you expect, so the rule is simple: numbers use ==, text uses equals.

The switch statement is a tidy way to choose between many fixed options for one value. It is handy for menus, days of the week, or single-character commands:

int day = 3;
switch (day) {
    case 1:
        System.out.println("Monday");
        break;
    case 2:
        System.out.println("Tuesday");
        break;
    case 3:
        System.out.println("Wednesday");
        break;
    default:
        System.out.println("Another day");
}

Each case labels one value, and break stops the switch after that case runs. Forgetting break is a famous bug: without it, Java falls through and runs the next case too, printing two days. The default case runs when no case matches, and it is good practice to always include one. Use switch when you are testing one value against a list of exact matches; use if and else if when the conditions are ranges or complex combinations.

Two mistakes to watch for in this lesson. First, the assignment-versus-comparison mix-up already mentioned: read = as gets and == as equals every time. Second, the semicolon after the if condition: writing if (age >= 18); with a semicolon ends the if statement right there, so the next block runs no matter what. The if line must not end with a semicolon. When a program seems to ignore your condition entirely, check for that stray semicolon first.

Practice: Write a program called TicketPrice.java. Declare an int age and a boolean isStudent, and set them to values you choose. Use if, else if, and else to compute a ticket price: children under 12 pay 5, students pay 8, adults aged 65 and over pay 7, and everyone else pays 12. Print the price. Then change the values of age and isStudent and run the program again several times, checking each branch in turn: a child, a student, an older adult, and a regular adult. As a second exercise, write a small switch program that takes an int from 1 to 7 and prints the day of the week, with a default for any other number, and test it with 1, 5, and 9.

This lesson teaches loops: code that repeats a block while a condition holds or for a fixed number of times. By the end you will write for loops and while loops, choose between them, control loops with break and continue, and use loops to process collections of values. Loops are what let a program handle ten items as easily as ten thousand, and they are the engine inside almost every real program.

A for loop is best when you know how many times you want to repeat, or you want to count through a range. Its header has three parts separated by semicolons: a starting value, a condition to keep going, and an update step:

for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
    System.out.println("Count: " + i);
}

This prints Count: 0 through Count: 4, five lines in total. The variable i starts at 0, the loop runs while i < 5, and i++ adds one after each pass. That zero start surprises beginners, so remember it clearly: i < 5 with i starting at 0 gives five passes, 0 through 4. If you need 1 through 5, write for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++). The difference between < and <= is the classic off-by-one error: one extra or one missing pass. When a loop runs the wrong number of times, add a temporary print of i inside it and watch the actual values; seeing the sequence usually reveals the mistake at once. The loop variable i exists only inside the loop, so you cannot use it after the closing brace.

A while loop repeats as long as its condition stays true, which makes it ideal when you do not know in advance how many repetitions you need:

int attempts = 0;
int secret = 7;
int guess = 0;
while (guess != secret) {
    guess = 7;
    attempts++;
}
System.out.println("Guessed in " + attempts + " tries.");

In a real program the guess would come from user input, which you will add in the practice exercise. The danger with while is the infinite loop: if the condition can never become false, the loop runs forever. Always make sure something inside the loop body moves the condition toward false. In the example, guess changes each pass. If you ever get stuck in an infinite loop, stop the program in your terminal and check what should have changed the condition but did not. A do-while loop is a small variation that runs the body once before checking the condition, which is handy for menus that must show at least once:

int choice;
do {
    System.out.println("Menu: 1) Play 2) Quit");
    choice = 1;
} while (choice != 2);

Two tools give you finer control inside any loop: break exits the loop immediately, and continue skips the rest of the current pass and jumps to the next one. For example, break can end a guessing game the moment the player wins, and continue can skip zero values while processing a list of numbers. Use them sparingly and clearly: a loop with many breaks scattered through it is hard to read, so prefer a clear condition when you can.

Loops become powerful when they accumulate a result. A common pattern is to start a total at zero, loop through values, and add each one:

int[] scores = {85, 92, 78, 90};
int total = 0;
for (int i = 0; i < scores.length; i++) {
    total += scores[i];
}
System.out.println("Total: " + total);
System.out.println("Average: " + (total / 4.0));

The array in this example is a preview of Lesson 6: a list of values held under one name, read by position. The loop visits each position in turn. Notice the average divides by 4.0, not 4, so the result is a decimal: dividing two ints would drop the fraction, as Lesson 2 warned. Another common pattern is searching: loop through values, and when you find the one you want, remember its position and break. A third is counting matches: start a counter at zero and add one each time a value meets a condition. Learn these three patterns, total, search, and count, and you will recognize them in code for years.

Two mistakes dominate this lesson. First, the off-by-one error already mentioned: i <= scores.length runs one pass too many and tries to read past the end of the array, which crashes with an index error. The correct bound is i < scores.length. Second, forgetting to update the loop variable in a while loop, or updating it in the wrong place, which creates an infinite loop. A good habit is to write the update step immediately after writing the while line, before filling in the rest of the body.

Practice: Write a program called TimesTable.java that uses a for loop to print the 7-times table from 7 x 1 to 7 x 12, one line per row. Then write a second program called GuessingGame.java that picks a secret number in the code, uses a while loop to keep asking the user for a guess until they get it right, prints Too high or Too low after each wrong guess using if and else if, counts the attempts, and prints how many tries it took when they win. To read user input, use a Scanner: import java.util.Scanner; at the top, create Scanner input = new Scanner(System.in); before the loop, read guesses with int guess = input.nextInt(); inside the loop, and close the scanner with input.close(); at the end. Test the game with guesses below, above, and equal to the secret.

This lesson teaches you to package code into methods: named, reusable blocks that take inputs and produce outputs. By the end you will define methods with parameters and return values, call them from main and from each other, and understand the difference between printing a value and returning it. You will also learn about method overloading and variable scope. Methods are the single most important organizing tool in programming: they let you write a piece of logic once, test it once, and reuse it everywhere without copying and pasting.

You have already been using methods: System.out.println and String.equals are methods that someone else wrote. Defining your own looks like this, placed inside the class but outside main:

public static double celsiusToFahrenheit(double celsius) {
    return (celsius * 9.0 / 5.0) + 32.0;
}

The first word, public, means the method can be used from other classes. The word static means it belongs to the class rather than to one particular object; Lesson 7 explains that distinction, and for now every method in this course is static so it can be called from main. The word double before the name is the return type: the kind of value this method gives back. The name follows Java naming style, starting with a lowercase letter and capitalizing each new word, which is called camel case. The parentheses hold parameters: the inputs the method expects, each with a type and a name. The body computes the result, and the return statement sends it back to the caller. Calling it looks like this:

double result = celsiusToFahrenheit(25.0);
System.out.println(result);

The argument 25.0 is passed in as the parameter celsius, the method computes 77.0, and that value is stored in result. A method can take several parameters, separated by commas, and the arguments you pass must match in number, order, and type. A method that returns nothing uses the return type void, like main itself, and it ends without a return statement, or with a bare return; to exit early.

The difference between printing and returning is the key idea of this lesson. Compare two versions of an addition method. One prints: public static void showSum(int a, int b) { System.out.println(a + b); }. The other returns: public static int add(int a, int b) { return a + b; }. The printing version shows 7 on the screen but gives nothing back, so the caller cannot use the 7 for anything else. The returning version hands 7 back, so the caller can store it, double it, or pass it to another method: int doubled = add(3, 4) * 2;. The rule of thumb is: print is for showing humans, return is for giving values back to your program so it can keep working with them. When your method computes something the rest of the program needs, a total, a converted value, a yes-or-no answer, return it. When it only needs to display something, print it. Methods can also call other methods, which is normal and encouraged: a method that describes a temperature can call celsiusToFahrenheit inside itself instead of repeating the formula.

Method overloading lets you define several methods with the same name but different parameter lists, and Java picks the right one by the arguments you pass. For example, you might have add(int a, int b) and add(double a, double b), or greet(String name) and greet(String name, String greeting). This keeps names simple while allowing flexibility. Parameters can also have sensible behavior built in: a method can check its inputs and handle edge cases, such as returning zero for a negative quantity, rather than trusting every caller to check first. Put that checking in one place, inside the method, and every caller benefits.

Scope determines where a variable can be seen. Variables created inside a method are local: they exist only while that method runs and are invisible outside it. Parameters are also local to their method. A variable declared in main cannot be seen inside another method, and a variable declared inside a helper method cannot be seen in main. This is a feature, not a limitation: it keeps methods independent, so you can understand and test each one on its own. Data flows into a method through its parameters and out through its return value, and that clear flow is what makes methods predictable. If two methods both need the same value, pass it as an argument to both rather than trying to share a variable between them.

Two mistakes to watch for. First, calling a method before you understand its return type: if a method returns a value and you ignore it, the work is wasted, and if a void method is used as if it returned something, the compiler will stop you. Always check the return type in the method header. Second, mismatched types in a call: passing a String where an int is expected, or passing two arguments to a method that takes three, are compile errors. Read the method header like a contract: it states exactly what goes in and what comes out, and the call must honor it.

Practice: Write a file called Converters.java with three static methods: celsiusToFahrenheit(double c) that returns (c * 9.0 / 5.0) + 32.0, milesToKm(double miles) that returns miles * 1.609, and describeTemperature(double c) that calls celsiusToFahrenheit and returns a String like "25.0C is 77.0F". Note how the third method calls the first: methods calling methods is normal. Then write a main method that asks the user for a Celsius temperature using a Scanner, converts the input, and prints the result of describeTemperature. To read a decimal, use input.nextDouble(). Test with 0, 25, and 100, and verify the outputs are 32.0, 77.0, and 212.0. As a stretch, add an overloaded describeTemperature that also takes a place name and returns a sentence naming the place.

This lesson teaches arrays: ordered collections that hold many values of the same type under one name. By the end you will create arrays, read and change items by position, find the length of an array, loop through arrays with for and enhanced for loops, and pass arrays to methods. Arrays are what let programs handle real quantities of data: instead of one variable per score, you keep a hundred scores in a single structure and process them with loops from Lesson 4.

An array is declared with square brackets after the type, and created with new and a size, or filled at once with values in braces:

int[] scores = new int[5];
int[] grades = {85, 92, 78, 90, 88};
String[] names = {"Ava", "Ben", "Cara"};

The first form creates an array of five ints, all starting at zero. The second form creates an array already holding those five numbers. Items are numbered starting at 0, so grades[0] is 85, grades[2] is 78, and grades[4] is 88. You read an item with the same bracket notation and change one by assigning to a position: grades[1] = 95; replaces 92 with 95. The length of an array is available as a field, not a method: grades.length is 5, with no parentheses. That distinction matters, because String has a length() method with parentheses while arrays use a length field without them, and mixing the two up is a common compile error.

Looping is where arrays shine. A standard for loop visits each position by index, which is what you need when the position itself matters:

for (int i = 0; i < grades.length; i++) {
    System.out.println("Score " + i + ": " + grades[i]);
}

An enhanced for loop, also called a for-each loop, visits each value directly and is simpler when you only need the values:

for (int grade : grades) {
    System.out.println("Score: " + grade);
}

Read the second form as: for each grade in grades, do this. Use the standard loop when you need the index, for example to change items or to compare neighbors; use the enhanced loop when you only need to read values. Both patterns appear constantly in real code, so learn to read both fluently. A very common use is finding a total, a highest value, or an average: start a variable before the loop, update it inside, and use it after the loop, exactly as Lesson 4 showed with totals.

Arrays can be passed to methods just like single values, and methods can return arrays too. A method that computes an average might look like this:

public static double average(int[] values) {
    int total = 0;
    for (int value : values) {
        total += value;
    }
    return total / (double) values.length;
}

The parameter int[] values accepts any int array, so the same method works for five scores or five hundred. Notice the cast (double) before values.length: it converts the length to a double so the division gives a decimal result instead of dropping the fraction, as Lesson 2 warned. Inside a method, an array parameter refers to the same array the caller passed, so changing items inside the method changes them for the caller too. That shared behavior is useful but worth knowing: if a method should not change the caller's data, it should work on a copy.

Two-dimensional arrays hold tables of values, such as a grid of seats or a month of daily readings, and they are simply arrays of arrays:

int[][] grid = {
    {1, 2, 3},
    {4, 5, 6}
};

Here grid[0][1] is 2, the second item in the first row. You process them with nested loops: an outer loop for rows and an inner loop for columns. Two-dimensional arrays are not needed for the final project, but recognizing them will help when you meet real tables of data in later learning.

Two mistakes are classic here. First, the off-by-one index error: the last item of an array with five items is grades[4], not grades[5], and grades[5] crashes the program with an ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException. When in doubt, use grades[grades.length - 1] for the last item. Second, comparing arrays with == or equals and expecting it to compare contents: == on arrays checks whether they are the same array object, not whether they hold the same values. To compare contents element by element, loop through and compare each position, or use a library helper designed for that purpose.

Practice: Write a program called GradeBook.java. Create an int array with eight scores of your choice. Write a method findHighest that takes an int array and returns the largest value, a method findLowest that returns the smallest, and a method average that returns the average as a double. In main, print the highest, lowest, and average. Then add a loop that counts how many scores are 90 or above and prints that count. Test with your scores, then change two scores and run again to confirm the results update. As a stretch, create a String array of student names in the same order as the scores and print each name beside its score using one loop over the index.

This lesson teaches classes and objects: the heart of Java. By the end you will define a class with fields and methods, create objects from it with new, use constructors to set starting values, and understand how objects differ from the simple values you have used so far. You will also learn encapsulation, the practice of keeping an object's data safe behind methods. Classes let you model real things, a book, a bank account, a player, as a bundle of data plus the actions that belong to it, and that modeling skill is what makes larger programs manageable.

A class is a blueprint; an object is one thing built from that blueprint. You have already seen one class, Hello, and the main class of every program. Here is a small class that models a book:

public class Book {
    private String title;
    private String author;
    private int pages;

    public Book(String title, String author, int pages) {
        this.title = title;
        this.author = author;
        this.pages = pages;
    }

    public String getTitle() {
        return title;
    }

    public String getAuthor() {
        return author;
    }

    public int getPages() {
        return pages;
    }

    public String describe() {
        return title + " by " + author + " (" + pages + " pages)";
    }
}

The fields at the top, title, author, and pages, are the data each Book object holds. The word private means these fields can only be touched from inside the Book class itself, which protects them from accidental changes elsewhere. The Book method with the same name as the class is a constructor: it runs when an object is created and sets the starting values. Inside it, this.title means the field of this object, as opposed to the parameter title, which is why this is needed when the names match. The getTitle, getAuthor, and getPages methods are getters: they let other code read a field without changing it. The describe method returns a readable sentence about the book. Notice that describe and the getters have no static word: they belong to one particular object, and they work on that object's fields.

Creating and using an object looks like this, in a different class with a main method:

Book first = new Book("Dune", "Frank Herbert", 412);
System.out.println(first.describe());
System.out.println(first.getTitle());

The word new creates the object and runs the constructor with the arguments you pass. The variable first holds a reference to that object, and the dot notation calls its methods: first.describe() asks that specific book to describe itself. A second object, Book second = new Book("Emma", "Jane Austen", 320);, is independent: changing one book's data never affects the other, because each object holds its own fields. That independence is the point of objects. The main method is static, which means it belongs to the class, not to an object, so it cannot directly use non-static fields; it must create an object first and then work through it. That is why every program so far has had static methods called from main, and why this lesson's objects are created inside main before their methods are used.

Encapsulation is the discipline of keeping fields private and giving other code controlled access through methods. Why bother? Because methods can check and protect the data. A Book with negative pages makes no sense, so a setter for pages could refuse negative values:

public void setPages(int pages) {
    if (pages > 0) {
        this.pages = pages;
    }
}

Callers set pages through setPages, and bad values are rejected in one place instead of being checked everywhere the field is used. Getters and setters together are called accessors, and they are the standard Java style: fields private, access through methods. You will also meet the toString method, a special method Java calls automatically when an object is printed: if you define public String toString() returning a readable description, then System.out.println(first); prints that description instead of a cryptic memory reference. Defining toString is good practice for every class you write.

Objects can also hold other objects and arrays, which is how real models are built. A library catalog could be a class holding an array of Book objects, with methods to add a book and search by title. That is exactly the final project in Lesson 8, and it works because each Book knows how to describe itself while the catalog knows how to manage the collection. One more idea completes the picture: a class can have static fields and methods shared by all objects, such as a counter of how many books have been created, but most of your classes will use instance fields and methods like the ones above, with each object holding its own data.

Two mistakes to watch for. First, forgetting new: writing Book first; declares a variable but creates no object, and calling first.describe() on it crashes with a NullPointerException, because there is no object yet. Always create with new before using. Second, comparing objects with == when you mean to compare their contents: first == second checks whether they are the same object in memory, not whether they hold the same title and author. For Strings, use equals, as Lesson 3 taught, and for your own classes, compare the fields that matter, such as first.getTitle().equals(second.getTitle()).

Practice: Create a file called Book.java with the class shown above, including the describe method and the three getters. Then create a second file called LibraryTest.java with a main method that creates three Book objects with titles, authors, and page counts you choose, and prints each book's description using describe(). Then add a getPages call to print the longest book's page count by comparing the three values with if statements. Run the program and confirm all three descriptions print correctly. As a stretch, add a setPages method with the positive-value check shown above, call it with a negative number, and confirm the page count does not change.

This final lesson puts the whole course together in one project: a small book catalog program that stores books, lists them, and searches for a title. By the end you will have combined classes, arrays, loops, methods, conditionals, and user input in one working program, and tested it like a user would. It closes with a map of where to go next. Start every project by listing features in plain language before writing code, because a clear feature list turns a big task into small steps you already know how to take.

Our catalog needs four features. First, add a book: the user types a title, an author, and a page count, and the program stores a new Book object. Second, list all books, printing each description. Third, search by title and print the matching book, or a clear message when nothing matches. Fourth, quit cleanly. Each feature maps to a lesson: input with a Scanner from Lesson 4, conditionals from Lesson 3, loops and arrays from Lessons 4 and 6, methods from Lesson 5, and the Book class from Lesson 7. Asking which lesson taught this piece is how you break big projects down, and it is a habit that will serve you in every project after this course.

The design has two classes. Book is the class from Lesson 7, unchanged, with its private fields, constructor, getters, and describe method. Catalog is a new class that holds the collection and the operations on it. Because this course has not yet taught resizable collections, the catalog uses a fixed-size array with a count of how many slots are actually filled, a pattern you will meet often in beginner code:

public class Catalog {
    private Book[] books;
    private int count;

    public Catalog(int capacity) {
        books = new Book[capacity];
        count = 0;
    }

    public void addBook(Book book) {
        if (count < books.length) {
            books[count] = book;
            count++;
        } else {
            System.out.println("The catalog is full.");
        }
    }

    public void listBooks() {
        if (count == 0) {
            System.out.println("No books yet.");
            return;
        }
        for (int i = 0; i < count; i++) {
            System.out.println(books[i].describe());
        }
    }

    public Book findByTitle(String title) {
        for (int i = 0; i < count; i++) {
            if (books[i].getTitle().equalsIgnoreCase(title)) {
                return books[i];
            }
        }
        return null;
    }
}

Read this carefully, because every line uses a lesson skill. The constructor creates the array and sets count to zero. addBook stores the book in the next free slot and advances the count, but refuses when the catalog is full, instead of crashing. listBooks loops only over the filled slots, i < count, not the whole array, because the empty slots hold nothing. findByTitle loops through the filled books, compares titles with equalsIgnoreCase so capital letters do not matter, returns the match at once, and returns null when nothing matches. Returning null to mean not found is a standard convention, and the caller must check for it before using the result.

The main class ties it together with a menu loop:

public class CatalogApp {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Catalog catalog = new Catalog(20);
        java.util.Scanner input = new java.util.Scanner(System.in);
        while (true) {
            System.out.println("1) Add  2) List  3) Search  4) Quit");
            System.out.print("Choose: ");
            int choice = input.nextInt();
            input.nextLine();
            if (choice == 1) {
                System.out.print("Title: ");
                String title = input.nextLine();
                System.out.print("Author: ");
                String author = input.nextLine();
                System.out.print("Pages: ");
                int pages = input.nextInt();
                input.nextLine();
                catalog.addBook(new Book(title, author, pages));
            } else if (choice == 2) {
                catalog.listBooks();
            } else if (choice == 3) {
                System.out.print("Search title: ");
                String title = input.nextLine();
                Book found = catalog.findByTitle(title);
                if (found == null) {
                    System.out.println("No book with that title.");
                } else {
                    System.out.println(found.describe());
                }
            } else if (choice == 4) {
                System.out.println("Goodbye!");
                break;
            } else {
                System.out.println("Unknown choice, try 1-4.");
            }
        }
        input.close();
    }
}

Two details deserve attention. After nextInt, the program calls input.nextLine() once to consume the leftover line break, because nextInt reads the number but leaves the Enter keystroke behind, and the next nextLine would otherwise read an empty line. That is a famous Scanner trap, and now you know the fix. Also notice the architecture: main owns the loop and the user interaction, Catalog owns the data and the operations, and Book owns its own description. Each class has one job, each method does one thing, and the program grows by adding small methods, not by rewriting.

Test the complete cycle like a user would: add three books, list them, search for one that exists, search for one that does not, and quit. Then add books until the catalog is full and confirm it reports that it is full instead of crashing. Two project-level mistakes to watch for: forgetting the null check after findByTitle, which crashes when a search misses, and looping over the whole array instead of only the filled count, which crashes on empty slots. Both are fixed by the patterns shown above.

Where to go next depends on what excited you. If modeling real things thrilled you, learn inheritance and interfaces, which let classes share behavior and define contracts. If data handling appealed to you, learn resizable collections and file handling so your catalog can save its books between runs. If you want clickable things, graphical interfaces and web frameworks turn Java into applications people use with a mouse. Whatever you choose, the method stays the same: small project, break into steps, test each step, read errors calmly. You have the complete foundation; the rest is practice.

Practice: Build the full catalog in three files, Book.java, Catalog.java, and CatalogApp.java, typing them yourself rather than copying. Test the complete cycle described above. Then add ONE extension of your choice: a method that counts the total pages in the catalog, a search by author, or a method that prints only books over a given page count. Verify it with fresh test data. When it all works, you have finished the course: a working, object-based, menu-driven program written entirely by you.

This lesson teaches you the building blocks every JavaScript program is made of: the core data types, the operators that combine them, and if/else statements for making decisions. By the end you will know the difference between strings, numbers, booleans, null, and undefined; you will use strict equality to avoid JavaScript's most famous trap; and you will write programs that choose different paths based on conditions. These ideas appear in every lesson after this one, so run each example in your console before moving on.

JavaScript has a small set of core types you will use constantly. A string is text in quotes: "hello", 'JavaScript', "42". A number covers both integers and decimals: 7, 3.5, -20 — JavaScript does not separate the two the way some languages do. A boolean is true or false, written lowercase. Then come the two "empty" values: null means "intentionally no value" (you set it on purpose), while undefined means "no value has been assigned yet" (JavaScript's default for untouched variables). The typeof operator tells you what you are holding: typeof "hello" gives "string", typeof 42 gives "number", typeof true gives "boolean". Types matter because JavaScript treats "42" (text) and 42 (a number) very differently, as you will see with operators next.

Arithmetic operators work like a calculator: + - * /, plus % for remainder (7 % 2 gives 1) and ** for powers (2 ** 3 gives 8). The + operator on strings joins them, called concatenation: "Good " + "morning" gives "Good morning". Here is JavaScript's famous trap: "5" + 3 gives "53", not 8, because + with a string converts the other side to text. To convert deliberately, use Number("5") to get 5 and String(42) to get "42". Now the equality trap: JavaScript has two kinds. Loose equality == converts types before comparing, so "5" == 5 is true — usually not what you want. Strict equality === compares without converting, so "5" === 5 is false. The rule professionals follow: always use === and !==, never == and !=, and an entire category of mysterious bugs disappears.

Comparison operators ask questions and answer with booleans: === checks equality, !== checks inequality, and < > <= >= compare size. Logical operators combine them: && means "and" (true only if both sides are true), || means "or" (true if either side is true), and ! flips a value. So age >= 18 && hasTicket gives true only when both hold. An if statement runs a block only when its condition is true:

const age = Number(prompt("Enter your age:"));
if (age >= 18) {
  console.log("You are an adult.");
} else {
  console.log("You are under 18.");
}

Note the parentheses around the condition and the curly braces marking each block — every indented statement between the braces belongs to that branch. For more paths, add else if branches: they are checked top to bottom and the first true one runs. The prompt() function here shows a pop-up asking for input and returns whatever was typed as a string, which is why we wrap it in Number() — the same string-input catch you would expect from any language.

Two mistakes dominate this lesson. First, using == instead of ===: "0" == false is true under loose equality, a genuinely baffling result that strict equality avoids entirely. If a comparison behaves impossibly, check which equality operator you used. Second, the assignment-in-condition slip: if (x = 5) assigns 5 to x and is always truthy, while if (x === 5) actually compares. Read = as "gets" and === as "is exactly" and your brain will flag the mix-up. A related gotcha: prompt() always returns a string, so comparing its result with a number using === fails unless you convert first with Number().

Practice: In your console, evaluate each of these and make sure you can explain the result before checking: "10" + 5, 10 + 5, Number("10") + 5, "5" === 5, "5" == 5, 0 === false, !true, and (7 > 3) && (2 < 1). Then create grade.html with a script that uses prompt() to ask for a test score, converts it with Number(), and logs "Grade: A" for 90+, "Grade: B" for 80+, "Grade: C" for 70+, and "Grade: below C" otherwise, using if/else if/else. Test it with 95, 82, and 64, and also test typing a non-number to see what happens — note the result, because handling that gracefully is a skill you will build on later.

This lesson teaches you the Document Object Model: JavaScript's live map of the web page, and the tool you use to change what visitors see. By the end you will select elements by id and with CSS-style selectors, read and change their text, adjust their styles, and create brand-new elements from code. The DOM is what turns JavaScript from a console curiosity into the language of interactive pages — every dynamic website you have ever used runs on these operations.

The DOM is the browser's tree-shaped representation of your HTML: every tag becomes a node you can reach from JavaScript. To work with an element, first give it an id in the HTML, then grab it:

<h1 id="title">Welcome</h1>
<p id="message">Loading...</p>

<script>
const titleEl = document.getElementById("title");
const msgEl = document.querySelector("#message");
console.log(titleEl.textContent);  // Welcome
</script>

document.getElementById grabs one element by its id, while document.querySelector takes any CSS selector — "#message" for an id, ".warning" for a class, "button" for a tag — and returns the first match (querySelectorAll returns all matches). Once you hold an element, textContent reads or sets its text: msgEl.textContent = "Hello, Ava!"; rewrites the paragraph instantly. Prefer textContent over innerHTML for plain text: innerHTML interprets the string as HTML, which is powerful but dangerous with user-supplied text, while textContent treats everything as literal characters and is always safe. innerHTML has legitimate uses — inserting your own static markup, like building a card from a template literal — but treat any string containing user input as textContent-only territory.

Changing styles is just as direct — every CSS property is available in camelCase on the element's style object:

titleEl.style.color = "darkblue";
titleEl.style.fontSize = "40px";
msgEl.classList.add("highlight");

Note backgroundColor instead of background-color: CSS's hyphens become camelCase in JavaScript. The classList approach is often cleaner than inline styles: define a .highlight class in a <style> block, then add or remove it with classList.add() and classList.remove(), or flip it with classList.toggle(). This keeps your styling in CSS where it belongs and your JavaScript focused on behavior — a separation professionals maintain strictly.

You can also build elements from scratch, which is how apps render lists of data:

const listEl = document.getElementById("tasks");
const item = document.createElement("li");
item.textContent = "Buy milk";
item.classList.add("task");
listEl.appendChild(item);

document.createElement("li") builds a new list item, you configure it like any selected element, and appendChild attaches it inside the list. This create-configure-attach pattern is the heart of dynamic pages: your data lives in arrays and objects (Lesson 3), and a render function turns that data into elements. You can set several properties before attaching — text, class, even nested children — and nothing appears on the page until appendChild runs, which lets you build complex structures offscreen and insert them in one step. The capstone app in Lesson 8 is essentially this pattern plus events.

Two mistakes dominate DOM work. First, running your script before the elements exist: if the script tag sits in the head and tries getElementById("title"), it gets null because the body has not loaded yet. The simple fix is placing your script tag at the end of the body, right before </body>, so all elements exist when it runs. Second, calling methods on null: when a selector finds nothing, you get null, and null.textContent crashes with "cannot read properties of null". When that error appears, your selector is wrong or the element is missing — console.log the variable first and confirm it holds an element before touching it.

Practice: Create profile.html with an h1 (id="name"), a paragraph (id="bio"), and an empty ul (id="hobbies"). Write a script at the end of the body that: sets the h1's text to your name, sets the bio paragraph to two sentences about you, changes the h1's color, and creates three li elements from an array of your hobbies ["reading", "cycling", "cooking"] using a for...of loop with createElement and appendChild. Open the page and confirm everything renders. Then, in the console, try document.querySelector("#does-not-exist") and observe the null — then try setting .textContent on it to see the exact error, so you recognize it instantly in future lessons.

This lesson teaches your pages to respond to users through events: the browser's way of announcing that something happened. By the end you will listen for clicks, react to typing as it happens, handle form submissions without reloading the page, and read information from the event object. Events are what make pages feel alive — without them, Lesson 4's DOM skills can only produce static pages that never react.

An event listener waits for something to happen on an element, then runs your function:

<button id="likeBtn">Like (0)</button>
<script>
const btn = document.getElementById("likeBtn");
let likes = 0;
btn.addEventListener("click", function () {
  likes = likes + 1;
  btn.textContent = "Like (" + likes + ")";
});
</script>

addEventListener takes the event name ("click") and a callback function that runs each time the event fires. The variable likes persists between clicks because it lives outside the callback — each click reads the current value, adds one, and rewrites the button's text. This pattern (state in a variable, event updates state, DOM re-renders) underlies every counter, toggle, and like button on the web. You can attach multiple listeners to the same element, and one callback can update several elements at once.

The input event fires on every keystroke inside a text field, which enables live feedback like character counters:

<label>Your message: <input id="msg" type="text"></label>
<p id="count">0 / 140</p>
<script>
const field = document.getElementById("msg");
const count = document.getElementById("count");
field.addEventListener("input", function () {
  count.textContent = field.value.length + " / 140";
});
</script>

The .value property holds what the user typed. Reading it inside the input handler means the count updates with every keystroke — try it and watch. This immediate loop (user acts, event fires, page updates) is the signature feel of modern web apps, and it is built entirely from pieces you already know: selection, properties, and text updates.

Forms need one special technique. A form's submit event reloads the page by default, wiping your JavaScript state — to handle submission in code, you must cancel that default behavior:

<form id="taskForm">
  <label>New task: <input id="taskInput" type="text"></label>
  <button type="submit">Add</button>
</form>
<ul id="taskList"></ul>
<script>
const form = document.getElementById("taskForm");
const input = document.getElementById("taskInput");
const list = document.getElementById("taskList");
form.addEventListener("submit", function (event) {
  event.preventDefault();
  const text = input.value.trim();
  if (text === "") { return; }
  const item = document.createElement("li");
  item.textContent = text;
  list.appendChild(item);
  input.value = "";
});
</script>

The event object, received as the callback's parameter, describes what happened; event.preventDefault() stops the browser's default reload. We trim whitespace and ignore empty submissions, create the list item with Lesson 4's pattern, and clear the field for the next entry. This is a miniature version of the capstone app — the only things missing are persistence and delete buttons, both coming in Lesson 8.

Two mistakes are classic here. First, forgetting preventDefault on submit: the page reloads, your added items vanish, and it looks like the code "didn't work" — it did work, then the reload erased it. If form handling ever seems to do nothing, check for preventDefault first. Second, attaching a listener to null because the id in getElementById does not match the HTML — ids are case-sensitive, so "taskForm" and "taskform" are different. When a button "does nothing," console.log the element right after selecting it; null means the selection failed, not the listener.

Practice: Create counter.html with three buttons ("Decrease", "Reset", "Increase") and a paragraph showing a number starting at 0. One listener per button should update the number and re-render the paragraph — decrease must not go below 0 (use an if). Then create signup.html with a form containing a name field and an email field plus a paragraph for messages. On submit, prevent the default reload; if either field is empty, show "Please fill in both fields." in the message paragraph; otherwise show "Thanks, <name>! We will write to <email>." and clear the form. Test every path, including submitting with empty fields and watching the page stay put.

This lesson teaches the modern JavaScript features that make code shorter and clearer: arrow functions, template literals, and destructuring, plus a few companion tools. By the end you will rewrite callbacks as one-line arrows, build strings with embedded expressions instead of messy concatenation, and pull values out of objects and arrays in a single statement. You will see these features in nearly every real-world codebase, so fluency here is what makes other people's code readable to you.

Arrow functions are a compact way to write functions, and they shine as event callbacks:

// traditional
btn.addEventListener("click", function () {
  console.log("clicked");
});

// arrow
btn.addEventListener("click", () => {
  console.log("clicked");
});

The () => syntax replaces function (). With a single parameter the parentheses are optional — x => x * 2 — and with a single expression the braces and return are implied: const double = x => x * 2; means "take x, return x * 2". Multi-line arrows keep braces and an explicit return. One caution: arrows handle the special this value differently from traditional functions, treating it as the surrounding context instead of the element that fired the event. For the event handlers in this course you do not need this at all — but if you ever copy code that uses this inside a handler, know that switching it to an arrow can change its meaning, so leave traditional functions in place there.

Template literals replace fiddly string concatenation. They use backticks instead of quotes, and ${} embeds any expression:

const name = "Ava";
const unread = 3;
const msg = `Hello, ${name}! You have ${unread} unread messages.`;
console.log(msg);  // Hello, Ava! You have 3 unread messages.

Compare that with "Hello, " + name + "! You have " + unread + " unread messages." — the template version reads like the final sentence. The ${} can hold any expression: ${unread + 1}, ${user.name.toUpperCase()}. Backticks also allow multi-line strings, so HTML snippets stay readable: const card = `<div class="card"><h2>${title}</h2><p>${body}</p></div>`;. From now on, prefer templates whenever a string contains variables.

Destructuring unpacks values in one move. From objects:

const user = { name: "Ava", age: 30, city: "Austin" };
const { name, age } = user;
console.log(name);  // Ava

The variable names must match the keys. From arrays, position matters instead: const [first, second] = scores; grabs the first two items, and you can skip with commas: const [, runnerUp] = scores;. Function parameters destructure too — function showUser({ name, city }) — which makes it obvious which properties a function needs. Combined with the spread operator (...), you get effortless copies and merges: const updated = { ...user, age: 31 }; copies user with a new age, and const all = [...listA, ...listB]; joins arrays. Spread copies are shallow but perfectly adequate for the data shapes in this course.

Two mistakes catch beginners here. First, using regular quotes with ${}: "Hello, ${name}" prints the literal characters ${name} — interpolation only works inside backticks, so when variables refuse to fill in, check the quote character. Second, destructuring a missing property gives undefined silently: const { email } = user where user has no email key leaves email undefined, and using it later crashes far from the real cause. When a destructured value is unexpectedly undefined, verify the key name against the object — spelling and case must match exactly.

Practice: Rewrite the like-button code from Lesson 5 using an arrow function and a template literal for the button text (`Like (${likes})`). Then, in the console, practice destructuring: create const order = { id: 101, item: "headphones", price: 59.99, qty: 2 }; and use destructuring plus a template literal to log "Order 101: 2 x headphones = $119.98" with the total computed as ${price * qty} inside the string. Next, merge two arrays with spread and log the result. Finally, convert one of your Lesson 3 functions to an arrow function assigned to a const, call it, and confirm identical behavior — this cements when arrows are a safe swap.

This lesson teaches your pages to load data from the web: the skill behind every feed, search result, and live dashboard you have ever used. By the end you will understand JSON as a data format, request it with the Fetch API, handle the asynchronous wait for a response, and render what comes back into your page. Fetching turns your apps from closed boxes into connected ones — the capstone's tasks come from the user, but the same techniques display data from anywhere.

JSON (JavaScript Object Notation) is the standard format for data on the web, and it looks like JavaScript objects and arrays written as text:

{
  "id": 7,
  "title": "Write report",
  "done": false,
  "tags": ["work", "urgent"]
}

Keys are quoted strings, values can be strings, numbers, booleans, null, arrays, or nested objects. JavaScript converts between JSON text and real values with two functions: JSON.parse(text) turns a JSON string into an object you can use, and JSON.stringify(value) turns an object back into text (useful for storage, as Lesson 8 will show). When a server sends data, it arrives as JSON text; your first job is always to parse it into something workable.

The Fetch API requests data from a web address. Because the response takes time to arrive, fetch does not return the data directly — it returns a promise, a stand-in object representing "the result, when it comes". The modern way to wait for it is async/await:

async function loadTasks() {
  try {
    const response = await fetch("https://example.com/api/tasks");
    if (!response.ok) {
      throw new Error("Server responded with " + response.status);
    }
    const tasks = await response.json();
    console.log(tasks);
  } catch (error) {
    console.log("Could not load tasks:", error.message);
  }
}
loadTasks();

Walk through it: await fetch(...) pauses the function until the response headers arrive; response.ok is false for errors like 404, so we check it and throw our own error rather than parsing garbage; await response.json() pauses again while the body downloads and parses; any failure at any step lands in catch. The try/catch from Lesson 6's error philosophy applies directly — network requests fail for ordinary reasons (no connection, wrong address, server down), so every fetch needs a catch. Note the example address is illustrative: replace it with a real JSON endpoint when you experiment, such as a public demo API that returns sample data.

Rendering fetched data uses your DOM skills in a loop. Suppose the endpoint returns an array of task objects with title and done properties:

async function renderTasks() {
  const listEl = document.getElementById("tasks");
  try {
    const response = await fetch("https://example.com/api/tasks");
    if (!response.ok) { throw new Error("Bad response: " + response.status); }
    const tasks = await response.json();
    listEl.textContent = "";
    for (const task of tasks) {
      const item = document.createElement("li");
      item.textContent = task.done ? "Done: " + task.title : task.title;
      listEl.appendChild(item);
    }
  } catch (error) {
    listEl.textContent = "Could not load tasks. Check your connection.";
  }
}

This is the full professional pattern: clear old content, fetch, validate, parse, loop and render — with a friendly message if anything fails. The ternary (condition ? a : b) is a compact if/else for choosing between two values, handy inside rendering code. Notice the user never sees a crash or a blank page: failure produces an honest message, which is how real apps behave.

Three mistakes are classic here. First, forgetting await: without it, response is a promise, not a response, and response.ok is undefined — if your fetch "returns nothing useful," check for missing await. Second, skipping the response.ok check and calling response.json() on an error page, which throws a confusing parse error instead of telling you the address was wrong. Third, trying to use fetched data outside the async function before it arrives — code after loadTasks() runs immediately, not after the data lands. Keep all data-dependent work inside the async function (or in functions it calls) until this timing feels natural.

Practice: Create fetch-demo.html with an empty ul (id="posts") and a paragraph (id="status"). Write an async function that fetches from a real public JSON demo endpoint of your choice (search for "public JSON demo API" — several free ones return sample posts with title fields), checks response.ok, parses the JSON, and renders the first five titles as list items. Show "Loading..." in the status paragraph before the fetch and clear it on success, or show an error message on failure. Test twice: once with the correct address, once with a deliberately wrong address, and confirm the failure case shows your friendly message instead of a console-only error.

This final lesson puts the whole course together in one capstone project: a complete interactive to-do app that adds tasks, marks them complete, deletes them, and saves everything in the browser so tasks survive reloads. By the end you will have combined DOM selection, events, modern JavaScript, and persistent storage in one page, and tested it like a user would. It closes with a map of where to go next.

Start every project by listing features in plain language before writing code. Our app needs five: 1) add a task from a form; 2) display all tasks as a list; 3) toggle a task complete/incomplete; 4) delete a task; 5) save tasks in the browser and reload them at startup. Each maps to a lesson: forms and events (Lesson 5), DOM creation (Lesson 4), arrays of objects (Lesson 3), template literals and arrows (Lesson 6), JSON for storage (Lesson 7). Asking which lesson taught a piece is how you break big projects down.

The data layer is an array of task objects plus two persistence functions. localStorage is a small key-value store built into every browser that survives page reloads — but it only stores strings, so we convert with JSON:

const STORAGE_KEY = "my-tasks-v1";

function loadTasks() {
  try {
    const saved = localStorage.getItem(STORAGE_KEY);
    return saved ? JSON.parse(saved) : [];
  } catch (error) {
    return [];
  }
}

function saveTasks(tasks) {
  localStorage.setItem(STORAGE_KEY, JSON.stringify(tasks));
}

Each task is { text: "Buy milk", done: false }. loadTasks handles the first visit (nothing saved returns []) and even corrupted data (parse failure returns [] instead of crashing). Test in the console by saving a sample array and loading it back.

Rendering turns the data into elements, and every user action follows the same cycle: update the array, save it, re-render:

let tasks = loadTasks();
const listEl = document.getElementById("taskList");

function render() {
  listEl.textContent = "";
  tasks.forEach((task, index) => {
    const item = document.createElement("li");
    const label = document.createElement("span");
    label.textContent = task.text;
    if (task.done) { label.style.textDecoration = "line-through"; }
    const toggleBtn = document.createElement("button");
    toggleBtn.textContent = task.done ? "Undo" : "Done";
    toggleBtn.addEventListener("click", () => {
      tasks[index].done = !tasks[index].done;
      saveTasks(tasks);
      render();
    });
    const delBtn = document.createElement("button");
    delBtn.textContent = "Delete";
    delBtn.addEventListener("click", () => {
      tasks = tasks.filter((t, i) => i !== index);
      saveTasks(tasks);
      render();
    });
    item.append(label, toggleBtn, delBtn);
    listEl.appendChild(item);
  });
}

document.getElementById("taskForm").addEventListener("submit", (event) => {
  event.preventDefault();
  const input = document.getElementById("taskInput");
  const text = input.value.trim();
  if (text === "") { return; }
  tasks.push({ text: text, done: false });
  saveTasks(tasks);
  render();
  input.value = "";
});

render();

Notice the architecture: tasks is the single source of truth, render() is the only code that touches the DOM list, and every change (add, toggle, delete) updates the array, saves, then re-renders. The filter() call for deletion keeps every task except the clicked index — a pattern worth memorizing.

Two capstone-level mistakes to watch for. First, forgetting to call saveTasks after a change: toggling works on screen but reloads lose it — the rule is that every function mutating tasks saves before returning. Second, stale indexes: render() rebuilds all buttons fresh each time, so each button's closure captures the correct index. Updating elements in place instead would let indexes drift and toggle the wrong task — which is why the rebuild pattern is recommended.

Natural extensions, each an evening's work: an All / Active / Completed filter, an edit button, a remaining-tasks counter, or due dates. Each reuses the same architecture — update the array, save, re-render — which is the real lesson: apps grow by adding small pieces, not by rewriting.

Where to go next depends on what excited you. If interactivity thrilled you, deepen DOM and event skills, then explore a UI framework like React or Vue, which organize bigger apps around the same state-render ideas. If data appealed to you, combine Lesson 7's fetching with rendering for live-data apps — weather displays, search tools, dashboards. If you want JavaScript outside the browser, Node.js lets the same language power servers and pairs naturally with Python backends like Flask. Whatever you choose, the method stays the same: small project, break into steps, test each step, read console errors calmly. You have the complete foundation; the rest is practice.

Practice: Build the full app in tasks.html from the feature list above, typing it yourself. Test the complete cycle: add three tasks, toggle one complete, delete another, reload the page, and confirm the remaining tasks survived (open DevTools > Application > Local Storage to see the saved JSON). Then add ONE extension of your choice — the remaining-tasks counter is the most instructive — and verify it updates on every action. When it all works, you have finished the course: a working, persistent, interactive web app written entirely by you.

This final lesson puts the whole course together in one capstone project: a command-line expense tracker that adds expenses, lists them, shows totals, and saves everything to a file so nothing is lost when the program closes. By the end you will have combined functions, loops, dictionaries, JSON storage, and error handling in one file, and tested it like a user would. It closes with a map of where to go next.

Start every project by listing features in plain language before writing code. Our expense tracker needs four: 1) add an expense (description, amount, category); 2) list all expenses; 3) show the total spent, and totals per category; 4) save to a JSON file on every change and load it at startup. Each maps to a lesson: input() and functions (Lessons 2 and 4), the menu loop (Lesson 3), a list of dictionaries (Lesson 5), JSON and try/except (Lesson 6). Asking "which lesson taught this piece?" is how you break big projects down.

Here is the data layer, the foundation everything else uses:

import json

DATA_FILE = "expenses.json"

def load_expenses():
    try:
        with open(DATA_FILE, "r", encoding="utf-8") as f:
            return json.load(f)
    except FileNotFoundError:
        return []

def save_expenses(expenses):
    with open(DATA_FILE, "w", encoding="utf-8") as f:
        json.dump(expenses, f, indent=2)

Each expense is a dictionary like {"description": "groceries", "amount": 42.50, "category": "food"}, and the whole tracker is a list of those dictionaries. load_expenses handles the first-run case by returning an empty list when the file does not exist yet — the exact pattern from Lesson 6. Keep these two functions small and correct before building anything on top of them; test them in the interactive prompt by saving a sample list and loading it back.

The feature functions operate on the in-memory list, and the menu loop ties it all together:

def add_expense(expenses):
    description = input("Description: ")
    try:
        amount = float(input("Amount: "))
    except ValueError:
        print("That is not a valid number — expense not added.")
        return
    category = input("Category: ")
    expenses.append({"description": description, "amount": amount, "category": category})
    save_expenses(expenses)
    print("Added.")

def show_total(expenses):
    total = sum(e["amount"] for e in expenses)
    print("Total spent:", round(total, 2))

def main():
    expenses = load_expenses()
    while True:
        print("\n1) Add  2) List  3) Total  4) Quit")
        choice = input("Choose: ")
        if choice == "1":
            add_expense(expenses)
        elif choice == "2":
            for e in expenses:
                print(e["description"], "-", e["amount"], "(" + e["category"] + ")")
        elif choice == "3":
            show_total(expenses)
        elif choice == "4":
            print("Goodbye!")
            break
        else:
            print("Unknown choice, try 1-4.")

main()

Notice the architecture: main() owns the loop and the data, each feature function does one job, and saving happens inside add_expense right after the change, so data survives an unexpected quit. Error handling sits exactly where user input enters — the float conversion — not wrapped around the whole program.

Two capstone-level mistakes to watch for. First, forgetting to save after a change — if listing works but quitting loses data, you are displaying the in-memory list fine but never called save_expenses after adding. A good rule: every function that mutates the data saves it before returning. Second, letting main() sprawl: if your loop body grows longer than a screen, extract a function — the add_expense/show_total split is the pattern to copy.

Natural extensions, each an evening's work: per-category totals, deleting an expense by number, editing an entry, or a monthly report filtered by a "date" field you add to each expense. Each reuses the same architecture — programs grow by adding small functions, not by rewriting.

Where to go next depends on what excited you. If automation thrilled you, learn the pathlib module, then scheduling scripts to run automatically. If data handling appealed to you, the csv module and then the pandas library open up spreadsheet-style analysis. If you want clickable things, web frameworks like Flask or Django turn Python into websites — the JavaScript course in this catalog pairs naturally. Whatever you choose, the method stays the same: small project, break into steps, test each step, read errors calmly. You have the complete foundation; the rest is practice.

Practice: Build the full expense tracker in expenses.py from the feature list above, typing it yourself rather than copying. Test the complete cycle: add three expenses, list them, check the total, quit, run the program again, and confirm your expenses survived (open expenses.json in your editor to see them). Then add ONE extension of your choice — per-category totals is the most instructive — and verify it with fresh test data. When it all works, you have finished the course: a working, data-persisting, user-friendly program written entirely by you.

This lesson teaches you CSS: the language that controls how your HTML looks. By the end you will write selectors that target elements, classes, and ids; set colors, fonts, and spacing; link a stylesheet to your pages; and predict layout using the box model. HTML is the skeleton; CSS is everything visual. The two stay in separate files so you can redesign a whole site without touching its content.

CSS rules have three parts: a selector (what to style), a property (what aspect), and a value. p { color: navy; } makes every paragraph navy. Write CSS in a separate file — styles.css in your my-site folder — and link it from each page's <head>: <link rel="stylesheet" href="styles.css">. One stylesheet shared by all pages means one place to change your site's look. (You can also write CSS inside a <style> tag in the head while experimenting, but move it to the file for real projects.)

Selectors, from broad to precise. Element selectors target every instance: h1 { ... }, p { ... }. Class selectors target any element with that class — reusable styles: .highlight { background: yellow; } applies to <p class="highlight"> and any other tagged element. Id selectors target one unique element: #main-title { ... } for <h1 id="main-title"> — use ids sparingly, mostly as JavaScript hooks (Lesson 6). Combine and refine: nav a { ... } styles links inside navigation only; h2.section-title targets h2 elements with that class; a:hover styles links when the mouse is over them. When two rules conflict, the more specific selector wins, and equal specificity goes to the later rule — this cascade is where the name "Cascading Style Sheets" comes from.

Colors and text. Set colors with names (navy, tomato), hex codes (#1a73e8 — six digits, pairs for red/green/blue), or rgb() values. color sets text color; background-color sets the fill behind it. Typography: font-family: Georgia, serif; lists fallback fonts in order — always end with a generic family (serif, sans-serif, monospace) so the page degrades gracefully. font-size: 18px; sets size (px is precise; Lesson 4 introduces flexible units), font-weight: bold;, line-height: 1.6; gives breathing room between lines, and text-align: center; centers text. Keep body text at a comfortable size with generous line height — readability beats decoration.

The box model governs all layout. Every element is a box with four layers, outside in: margin (space outside the box, separating it from neighbors), border (the edge), padding (space inside, between the border and the content), and the content itself. So this rule:

.card {
  width: 300px;
  padding: 20px;
  border: 2px solid #333;
  margin: 16px;
}

creates a 300px content area with 20px of inner space, a 2px border, and 16px of separation from neighbors. The classic beginner shock: by default, width sets only the content width, so the visible box is wider than 300px. Fix it globally with the universal box-sizing rule, which every project should start with:

* { box-sizing: border-box; }

Now padding and border count inside the declared width — 300px means 300px total. Margins collapse vertically (two stacked 16px margins make 16px of space, not 32) — surprising once, then natural.

Two mistakes dominate this lesson. First, the stylesheet not loading: check the <link> tag's href path and filename spelling, confirm styles.css is saved, and hard-refresh the browser (Ctrl+Shift+R) to bypass the cache. Second, styles "not working" that are actually being overridden: a more specific selector or a later rule is winning — use Inspect, find your element, and read the crossed-out rules to see exactly which rule beat yours.

Practice: Create styles.css and link it from both your pages. Style the body with a readable font, comfortable line height, and a max-width of 800px centered with margin: 0 auto. Style your header with a background color and your nav links with spacing and a hover color. Build one .card class (padding, border, rounded corners with border-radius: 8px) and apply it to two sections. Then deliberately break something — misspell a property — and use Inspect to find the browser ignoring it. Fix it. Understanding failure is part of the skill.

This lesson teaches you modern page layout: arranging elements in rows and columns with flexbox, and making pages adapt to phones, tablets, and desktops with responsive design. By the end you will build a navigation bar, a card grid, and a two-column layout with flexbox, and you will write media queries that reflow your site on small screens. Layout is where CSS turns from decoration into architecture.

Flexbox lays out the children of a container along one axis. Declare display: flex on the parent, and its children become flex items in a row. The essential properties: flex-direction (row or column — the main axis), justify-content (how items spread along the main axis: flex-start, center, space-between, space-around), align-items (how items line up on the cross axis: stretch, center, flex-start, flex-end), gap (space between items — far cleaner than margins), and flex-wrap (wrap lets items flow onto new lines instead of squishing). A navigation bar is the canonical example:

nav ul {
  display: flex;
  gap: 24px;
  list-style: none;
  padding: 0;
}

A card grid adds wrapping: .card-grid { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 16px; } with .card { flex: 1 1 250px; } — each card grows and shrinks but never below 250px, so you get as many columns as fit. A classic two-column layout: .layout { display: flex; gap: 24px; } with main { flex: 3; } and aside { flex: 1; } — the flex numbers divide available space proportionally (3:1). Centering, the ancient CSS riddle, is now trivial: display: flex; justify-content: center; align-items: center; on the parent.

Responsive design means one site that works everywhere, and it rests on three techniques. First, the viewport meta tag — without it, phones render your page at desktop width and shrink it: <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1"> goes in every page's head, always. Second, fluid sizing: images get max-width: 100%; height: auto; so they never overflow their container; layouts use percentages and flex instead of fixed pixel widths. Third, media queries — CSS rules that apply only under conditions, almost always max-width breakpoints:

/* phones: stack the columns */
@media (max-width: 700px) {
  .layout { flex-direction: column; }
  nav ul { flex-direction: column; gap: 8px; }
}

Design mobile-first when you can: write the base CSS for the small screen, then add min-width queries that enhance the layout as space grows. It is easier to add columns than to remove them, and it forces you to prioritize content.

Relative units make responsive typography smooth. px is absolute; em scales with the parent's font size (compounding in nested elements — a known trap); rem scales with the root font size (predictable — 1.5rem is always 1.5 times the base). Set body { font-size: 16px; } (or leave the browser default) and size headings in rem: h1 { font-size: 2rem; }. Percentages suit widths; rem suits type and spacing.

Two mistakes to watch for. First, forgetting the viewport meta tag — the number one cause of "my site looks tiny on my phone". Check every page's head. Second, fixed-width elements overflowing small screens: a 900px-wide image or table inside a 360px phone screen breaks the layout sideways. Apply max-width: 100% to images and test every page at phone width using your browser's device toolbar (in developer tools).

Practice: Rebuild your about.html layout with flexbox: a horizontal nav bar (logo left, links right — hint: margin-left: auto on the link group pushes it right), a two-column main/aside section, and a three-card row that wraps. Add the viewport meta tag if it is missing. Then write a max-width: 700px media query that stacks everything into one column and enlarges tap targets (nav links get padding: 12px). Test at desktop width and at phone width in the device toolbar, and fix every horizontal scrollbar you find.

This lesson teaches you JavaScript: the programming language that makes pages interactive. By the end you will declare variables, work with strings, numbers, and booleans, write functions, use conditionals and loops, and run code when the user clicks something. HTML is structure, CSS is appearance, JavaScript is behavior — the three layers of every page.

JavaScript runs inside a <script> tag, usually placed just before the closing </body> so the page content loads first:

<script>
  console.log("Hello from JavaScript!");
</script>

console.log prints to the browser console (in developer tools) — your window into what the code is doing. Use it constantly while learning: when unsure what a value is, log it.

Variables store values. Prefer const for values that will not change and let for values that will: const siteName = "My Site"; let visitorCount = 0; visitorCount = visitorCount + 1;. (Older code uses var — you will see it, but write const and let.) Data types: strings ("hello", 'hello' — text in quotes), numbers (42, 3.14 — no quotes), booleans (true/false), plus arrays (ordered lists: const colors = ["red", "green", "blue"];) and objects (named values: const user = { name: "Ava", age: 30 };). Template literals join strings cleanly with backticks: const greeting = `Hello, ${user.name}!`; — the ${} inserts the value.

Arrays and objects deserve a closer look because you will use them constantly. Arrays are zero-indexed: colors[0] is "red", colors[2] is "blue", and colors.length tells you how many items there are (3). Add to the end with colors.push("yellow"); — now length is 4. Objects bundle related values under names: user.name reads "Ava", user.age reads 30, and you can add new properties anytime with user.city = "Austin";. Bracket notation works too — user["name"] — which is useful when the property name itself is stored in a variable. Reach for arrays for lists of similar things (menu items, gallery images, form entries) and objects for single things with multiple attributes (a user, a product, a submitted form's data). You will combine them endlessly: an array of objects is how most real data looks.

Functions package reusable logic:

function greet(name) {
  return `Hello, ${name}!`;
}
console.log(greet("Ava")); // Hello, Ava!

Conditionals choose paths: if (age >= 18) { ... } else { ... }. Comparisons use === (strict equality — checks value and type) rather than ==, which does surprising type conversions beginners should avoid. Loops repeat: for (let i = 0; i < 3; i++) { console.log(i); } counts 0, 1, 2; arrays loop cleanly with for...of: for (const color of colors) { console.log(color); }.

Events connect code to the user. A button with an id, wired in JavaScript:

<button id="helloBtn">Say hello</button>
<script>
  const btn = document.getElementById("helloBtn");
  btn.addEventListener("click", function() {
    alert("Hello! You clicked the button.");
  });
</script>

addEventListener waits for the named event ("click", "submit", "input", "keydown") and runs your function when it happens. This pattern — find element, listen for event, run code — is the heartbeat of interactive pages, and Lesson 6 expands it into full DOM manipulation.

Two mistakes dominate this lesson. First, script running before the elements exist: if your <script> is in the <head> and tries getElementById on a button defined later in the body, it finds nothing. Put scripts at the end of the body (or wait for the DOMContentLoaded event). Second, the console is your diagnostic tool — when nothing happens, open it first. A red error message with a line number tells you exactly where to look; "Uncaught TypeError: Cannot read properties of null" almost always means getElementById found no element, usually a misspelled id.

Practice: Add a script to index.html that: (1) declares a const with your name and logs a greeting with a template literal; (2) defines a function add(a, b) that returns their sum and logs add(7, 5); (3) adds a button that, when clicked, asks for the visitor's name with prompt() and shows a personalized greeting with alert(). Open the console, click the button, and confirm each step works. Then intentionally misspell the button's id in the JavaScript, reload, read the console error, and fix it — reading errors calmly is a core developer skill.

This lesson teaches you the DOM (Document Object Model): the browser's live representation of your page that JavaScript can read and change. By the end you will find elements, change their text, styles, and classes, create new elements, and build interactive widgets like counters, toggles, and lists. This is where pages stop being documents and start being applications.

The DOM is a tree of nodes mirroring your HTML: document is the root, each tag is an element node, text inside is a text node. JavaScript reaches into this tree. The main finders: document.getElementById("price") grabs one element by its unique id; document.querySelector(".card") grabs the first element matching any CSS selector; document.querySelectorAll("li") grabs all matching elements as a list you can loop over. querySelector is the most flexible — any selector you learned in Lesson 3 works: "#menu a", "input[type=email]", ".faq p".

Once found, elements are yours to change. textContent reads or sets the text inside: const el = document.getElementById("msg"); el.textContent = "Saved!";. innerHTML sets HTML markup (powerful but risky with user input — prefer textContent for plain text). Styles change through the style property using camelCase names: el.style.backgroundColor = "lightgreen"; el.style.fontSize = "20px";. Better still, toggle classes and let CSS do the work: el.classList.add("hidden"); el.classList.remove("hidden"); el.classList.toggle("active"); — with .hidden { display: none; } in your stylesheet. Class toggling keeps styling in CSS where it belongs and makes state changes one clean line.

You can also build elements from nothing: document.createElement("li") makes a new list item, you set its textContent, then parent.appendChild(newItem) attaches it. Removing: parent.removeChild(child), or the simpler child.remove(). Reading user input: const value = document.getElementById("name").value; — .value holds what the user typed. Put it together and you have the classic interactive widget — a click counter:

<p>Clicks: <span id="count">0</span></p>
<button id="clickBtn">Click me</button>
<script>
  let clicks = 0;
  const countSpan = document.getElementById("count");
  document.getElementById("clickBtn").addEventListener("click", function() {
    clicks = clicks + 1;
    countSpan.textContent = clicks;
  });
</script>

A variable holds the state, the listener updates it, and the DOM displays it. Nearly every interactive feature — tabs, accordions, image galleries, task lists — is this pattern with more elements. Another staple: theme toggle. A button toggles a dark class on <body>, and CSS defines body.dark { background: #222; color: #eee; } — one classList.toggle line, whole page re-themed.

querySelectorAll shines for repeated widgets like FAQ accordions. Give each question button the class "faq-q" and each answer the class "faq-a" (hidden by default with CSS). One loop wires them all:

document.querySelectorAll('.faq-q').forEach(function(btn) {
  btn.addEventListener('click', function() {
    btn.nextElementSibling.classList.toggle('hidden');
  });
});

Each button toggles only its own answer — nextElementSibling finds the element right after the button in the HTML. This is event handling at scale: ten questions, one small loop, zero repeated code. Notice the Lesson 5 pattern recurring: find elements, listen for events, update state and display. The DOM rewards this uniformity — once the pattern clicks, every widget is a variation on it.

Two mistakes to watch for. First, stale references: if you replace an element's innerHTML, any variables pointing at its old children now point at detached nodes — re-query after structural changes. Second, adding listeners inside loops incorrectly or adding the same listener twice (double-firing handlers): attach each listener once, at setup time, and use event delegation (one listener on a parent, checking event.target) for lists of similar items.

Practice: Build a "reading list" widget on about.html: an input, an "Add book" button, and an empty <ul>. Clicking the button (or pressing Enter — listen for it) appends the input's text as a new <li> with a small "remove" button beside it that deletes its own item when clicked; clear the input after each add and ignore empty submissions. Then add a theme-toggle button that toggles a dark class on the body with matching CSS. Test every path: add three books, remove the middle one, toggle the theme twice, and submit empty input to confirm it is ignored.

This lesson teaches you to handle forms with JavaScript: intercepting submission, reading and validating input, showing helpful errors, and building a complete small project. By the end you will build a validated contact form and a working tip calculator. Forms are where users give your site data — getting them right is one of the most practical skills in web development.

Browsers validate forms for free (Lesson 2: required, type="email", minlength), but JavaScript validation gives you custom rules and friendlier errors. The key move: listen for the form's submit event and call event.preventDefault() to stop the browser's default page-reload submission, then run your own logic:

<form id="contactForm" novalidate>
  <label for="email">Email:</label>
  <input type="email" id="email">
  <span class="error" id="emailError"></span>
  <button type="submit">Send</button>
</form>
<script>
  const form = document.getElementById("contactForm");
  form.addEventListener("submit", function(event) {
    event.preventDefault();
    const email = document.getElementById("email").value.trim();
    const errorEl = document.getElementById("emailError");
    if (!email.includes("@") || !email.includes(".")) {
      errorEl.textContent = "Please enter a valid email address.";
      return;
    }
    errorEl.textContent = "";
    document.getElementById("thanks").textContent = "Thanks! We will reply to " + email;
    form.reset();
  });
</script>

(The novalidate attribute turns off the browser's built-in popups so your custom messages show instead — use one system, not both.) Validation principles: check on submit (and optionally as the user types, after the first error); show the error next to the field it belongs to; clear errors as soon as the input becomes valid; never trust client-side validation alone for anything important — real applications re-check on the server, but for this course the browser is the whole world.

Live validation — checking as the user types — makes forms feel responsive, but use it carefully. The input event fires on every keystroke, which is perfect for clearing an error the moment the input becomes valid, and terrible for flagging errors mid-word (nobody likes being told their half-typed email is wrong). The professional pattern: validate on submit, then once a field has shown an error, also validate it on input so the error clears the instant the user fixes it:

document.getElementById('email').addEventListener('input', function() {
  const value = this.value.trim();
  const ok = value.includes('@') && value.includes('.');
  document.getElementById('emailError').textContent = ok ? '' : 'Please enter a valid email address.';
});

Errors appear at submit and disappear as they are fixed — the form feels alive without nagging. Combine this with the submit handler above and you have validation users actually enjoy: strict where it matters, forgiving everywhere else.

Now the project: a tip calculator. Requirements in plain language first: user enters a bill amount and a tip percentage, clicks calculate, sees the tip and total; invalid input shows a friendly error instead of a weird number. HTML: two number inputs, a button, a result paragraph, an error span. JavaScript:

function calculateTip() {
  const bill = parseFloat(document.getElementById("bill").value);
  const percent = parseFloat(document.getElementById("percent").value);
  const errorEl = document.getElementById("tipError");
  if (isNaN(bill) || bill <= 0) {
    errorEl.textContent = "Enter a bill amount greater than zero.";
    return;
  }
  if (isNaN(percent) || percent < 0) {
    errorEl.textContent = "Enter a tip percentage of zero or more.";
    return;
  }
  errorEl.textContent = "";
  const tip = bill * (percent / 100);
  const total = bill + tip;
  document.getElementById("tipResult").textContent =
    `Tip: $${tip.toFixed(2)} — Total: $${total.toFixed(2)}`;
}
document.getElementById("calcBtn").addEventListener("click", calculateTip);

Note the guards: parseFloat turns text into numbers (isNaN catches non-numeric input), zero/negative bills are rejected with words, and toFixed(2) formats money to two decimals — floating-point math like 0.1 + 0.2 producing 0.30000000000000004 is a famous quirk, and toFixed is the standard fix for display.

Two mistakes to avoid. First, forgetting preventDefault and watching the page reload, wiping your results — if your form "flashes and resets", this is the cause. Second, doing math on strings: input values are always text, so "10" + "5" gives "105" (concatenation) instead of 15. Convert with parseFloat/parseInt or Number() before any arithmetic, and validate with isNaN.

Practice: Build the tip calculator as tip.html with the full validation above, styled with your stylesheet. Test the complete matrix: valid input (100, 15 → $15.00 / $115.00), empty inputs, text input ("abc"), zero bill, negative percentage — confirm each shows the right message and valid input clears errors. Then extend it once: add a "split between N people" input that also shows per-person amounts. When every case behaves, you have built a real interactive application.
